acy using Pyroom as the reference at maximum reflection order O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 . Lower is better for all metrics. \n\n Method \n\n CD \n\n ↓ \\downarrow \n\n NMSE \n\n ↓ \\downarrow dB \n\n EDC-Err \n\n ↓ \\downarrow dB \n\n RT60-Err \n\n ↓ \\downarrow ms \n\n DRR-Err \n\n ↓ \\downarrow dB \n\n MESH2IR \n 0.999 \n 0.51 \n 19.59 \n 235.67 \n 9.80 \n\n Full-ISM \n 3.40 × 10 − 7 3.40{\\times}10^{-7} \n -60.64 \n 0.002 \n 0.05 \n 0.003 \n\n PathRIR \n 0.141 \n -5.69 \n 4.69 \n 36.84 \n 0.54 \n\n PathRIR w/o \n\n Comp. MLP \n\n 0.181 \n -5.09 \n 18.60 \n 121.12 \n 2.88 \n\n Table 2 : Image-source-node reduction rate R img R_{\\mathrm{img}} and\nruntime speedup S rt S_{\\mathrm{rt}} of PathRIR \nfor O max = 1 O_{\\max}=1 – 10 10 . \n\n O max O_{\\max} \n R img R_{\\mathrm{img}} \n S rt ↑ S_{\\mathrm{rt}}\\uparrow \n\n ↑ \\uparrow % \n\n PathRIR \n\n vs. Pyroom \n\n PathRIR \n\n vs. Full-ISM \n\n w/o Comp. MLP \n\n vs. Full-ISM \n\n 1 \n 0.0 \n 1.28 \n 0.32 \n 0.66 \n\n 2 \n 0.0 \n 0.60 \n 0.62 \n 0.83 \n\n 3 \n 7.9 \n 0.22 \n 1.14 \n 1.25 \n\n 4 \n 30.8 \n 0.16 \n 3.75 \n 3.96 \n\n 5 \n 52.0 \n 0.19 \n 15.57 \n 16.22 \n\n 6 \n 67.2 \n 0.51 \n 73.18 \n 75.62 \n\n 7 \n 77.0 \n 2.16 \n 340.42 \n 349.37 \n\n 8 \n 83.4 \n 10.52 \n 1,767.16 \n 1,807.33 \n\n 9 \n 87.6 \n 53.57 \n 6,889.78 \n 7,020.47 \n\n 10 \n 90.5 \n 279.39 \n 42,133.45 \n 43,173.95 \n\n Fig. 2 : Example time-domain RIRs from Pyroom, PathRIR , and MESH2IR for the same configuration at O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 . \n\n Fig. 3 : Example EDCs from Pyroom, MESH2IR, and PathRIR with and without the Compensation-MLP at O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 . \n\n 4.4 Simulation efficiency evaluation \n\n For computational efficiency, we report image-source-node reduction rate R img R_{\\mathrm{img}} and runtime speedup S rt S_{\\mathrm{rt}} . The reduction rate R img R_{\\mathrm{img}} measures the fraction of image-source nodes removed relative to Full-ISM. The speedup S rt S_{\\mathrm{rt}} is measured separately relative to Pyroom and Full-ISM and includes Pruning-MLP inference, retained-path accumulation, and Compensation-MLP generation. \n\n As shown in Table  2 , PathRIR becomes faster than Full-ISM at O max = 3 O_{\\max}=3 and remains faster thereafter. It is consistently faster than Pyroom from O max = 7 O_{\\max}=7 onward. From O max = 4 O_{\\max}=4 onward, both R img R_{\\mathrm{img}} and S rt S_{\\mathrm{rt}} increase with the maximum order. At O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 , PathRIR removes 90.5 % 90.5\\% of image-source nodes and achieves speedups of 279.39 × 279.39\\times over Pyroom and 42 , 133.45 × 42{,}133.45\\times over Full-ISM, demonstrating its increasing computational benefit for high-order simulation. At lower orders, however, the smaller number of image-source computations provides less opportunity for pruning to offset the MLP inference and compensation overhead. \n\n 4.5 Ablation study \n\n We evaluate PathRIR without the Compensation-MLP, as reported in the last row of Table  1 and the last column of Table  2 . The pruning-only variant is faster at every tested order, but its advantage generally narrows as O max O_{\\max} increases. At O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 , it is approximately 2.5 % 2.5\\% faster than the full PathRIR ( 43 , 173.95 × 43{,}173.95\\times versus 42 , 133.45 × 42{,}133.45\\times over Full-ISM). Removing the Compensation-MLP, however, degrades all five acoustic metrics. CD increases from 0.141 to 0.181, while NMSE worsens from -5.69 dB to -5.09 dB. The degradation is greater for decay-related metrics: EDC-Err increases from 4.69 dB to 18.60 dB, RT60-Err from 36.84 ms to 121.12 ms, and DRR-Err from 0.54 dB to 2.88 dB. Figure  3 shows that the pruning-only EDC decays much faster than Pyroom because pruning removes many late-tail image sources. The Compensation-MLP mitigates this by adding an envelope-matched compensation tail to the pruned RIR. Although this tail does not recover the exact residual waveform, it helps PathRIR follow the Pyroom decay trend and reduces waveform- and decay-related errors at modest runtime cost. \n\n 5 Conclusion \n\n We presented PathRIR , a physics-guided framework for accelerating ISM-based RIR simulation while preserving the image-source structure of geometric acoustics. Instead of replacing the RIR simulator with a neural generator, PathRIR uses a Pruning-MLP to retain acoustically important paths and a lightweight Compensation-MLP to restore the missing late-tail energy envelope. Experiments on irregular 3D rooms show that its computational gains grow rapidly with O max O_{\\max} . At O max = 10 O_{\\max}=10 , PathRIR removes 90.5 % 90.5\\% of image-source nodes and achieves speedups of 279.39 × 279.39\\times over Pyroom and 42 , 133.45 × 42{,}133.45\\times over Full-ISM, while yielding lower errors than MESH2IR across all five acoustic metrics. The ablation shows that the Compensation-MLP improves all five metrics, with particularly large gains in decay-related accuracy, at modest runtime cost. Future work will explore ray-tracing or hybrid ISM/ray-tracing backends to better capture mid-to-late reverberation. \n\n References \n\n [1] \n J. B. Allen and D. A. Berkley (1979) \n\n Image method for efficiently simulating small-room acoustics .\n\n J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 65 ( 4 ), pp. 943–950 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 0001-4966 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [2] \n J. Borish (1984) \n\n Extension of the image model to arbitrary polyhedra .\n\n J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 75 ( 6 ), pp. 1827–1836 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 0001-4966 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 ,\n §4.1 .\n\n [3] \n J. S. Bradley (2011) \n\n Review of objective room acoustics measures and future needs .\n\n Appl. Acoust. 72 ( 10 ), pp. 713–720 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 0003-682X ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [4] \n A. Brunetto, S. Hornauer, and F. Moutarde (2025) \n\n NeRAF: 3D scene infused neural radiance and acoustic fields .\n\n In Proc. Int. Conf. Learn. Represent. (ICLR) ,\n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [5] \n C. Chen, C. Schissler, S. Garg, P. Kobernik, A. Clegg, P. Calamia, D. Batra, P. W. Robinson, and K. Grauman (2022) \n\n SoundSpaces 2.0: a simulation platform for visual-acoustic learning .\n\n In Adv. Neural Inf. Process. Syst. ,\n\n Vol. 35 , pp. 8896–8911 .\n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [6] \n S. Della Torre, M. Pezzoli, F. Antonacci, and S. Gannot (2025) \n\n DiffusionRIR: room impulse response interpolation using diffusion models .\n\n In Proc. Forum Acusticum / Euronoise ,\n\n pp. 4079–4086 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [7] \n D. Diaz-Guerra, A. Miguel, and J. R. Beltran (2021) \n\n gpuRIR: a Python library for room impulse response simulation with GPU acceleration .\n\n Multimedia Tools Appl. 80 ( 4 ), pp. 5653–5671 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 1380-7501 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [8] \n G. Götz, D. G. Nielsen, S. Gudjonsson, and F. Pind (2025) \n\n Room-acoustic simulations as an alternative to measurements for audio-algorithm evaluation .\n\n IEEE Access 13 , pp. 214000–214008 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 2169-3536 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [9] \n E. A. P. Habets (2006) \n\n Room impulse response generator .\n\n Tech. Rep. \n\n Technical Report 2.4 , Technische Universiteit Eindhoven .\n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [10] \n T. Ko, V. Peddinti, D. Povey, M. L. Seltzer, and S. Khudanpur (2017) \n\n A study on data augmentation of reverberant speech for robust speech recognition .\n\n In Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. (ICASSP) ,\n\n pp. 5220–5224 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [11] \n E. A. Lehmann and A. M. Johansson (2010) \n\n Diffuse reverberation model for efficient image-source simulation of room impulse responses .\n\n IEEE Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process. 18 ( 6 ), pp. 1429–1439 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 ,\n §3.4 .\n\n [12] \n S. Liang, C. Huang, Y. Tian, A. Kumar, and C. Xu (2023) \n\n Neural Acoustic Context Field: rendering realistic room impulse response with neural fields .\n\n In Proc. ICCV Workshop Visual Learn. Sounds Spaces (AV4D) ,\n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [13] \n Y. Luo and R. Gu (2024) \n\n Fast random approximation of multi-channel room impulse response .\n\n In Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. Workshops (ICASSPW) ,\n\n pp. 449–454 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [14] \n Y. Luo and J. Yu (2023) \n\n FRA-RIR: fast random approximation of the image-source method .\n\n In Proc. Interspeech ,\n\n pp. 3884–3888 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 2958-1796 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [15] \n D. R. Morgan, J. Benesty, and M. M. Sondhi (1998) \n\n On the evaluation of estimated impulse responses .\n\n IEEE Signal Process. Lett. 5 ( 7 ), pp. 174–176 .\n\n External Links: ISSN 1070-9908 ,\n Document \n\n Cited by: §4.3 .\n\n [16] \n T. Nakatani, T. Yoshioka, K. Kinoshita, M. Miyoshi, and B.-H. Juang (2010) \n\n Speech dereverberation based on variance-normalized delayed linear prediction .\n\n IEEE Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process. 18 ( 7 ), pp. 1717–1731 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [17] \n N. Raghuvanshi, R. Narain, and M. C. Lin (2009) \n\n Efficient and accurate sound propagation using adaptive rectangular decomposition .\n\n IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph. 15 ( 5 ), pp. 789–801 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [18] \n A. Ratnarajah, S. Ghosh, S. Kumar, P. Chiniya, and D. Manocha (2024) \n\n AV-RIR: audio-visual room impulse response estimation .\n\n In Proc. IEEE/CVF Conf. Comput. Vis. Pattern Recognit. (CVPR) ,\n\n pp. 27164–27175 .\n\n Cited by: §1 .\n\n [19] \n A. Ratnarajah, Z. Tang, R. Aralikatti, and D. Manocha (2022) \n\n MESH2IR: neural acoustic impulse response generator for complex 3D scenes .\n\n In Proc. 30th ACM Int. Conf. Multimedia (MM) ,\n\n pp. 924–933 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §1 ,\n §4.2 ,\n §4.3 .\n\n [20] \n A. Ratnarajah, Z. Tang, and D. Manocha (2021) \n\n IR-GAN: room impuls